{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAQrrbg95pcHPUqGhnJ8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`Hijack()` now only refuses once part of the response body has been written. Writing just the headers no longer blocks it. The full test suite passes.\n\n**The problem:** an earlier change (#4295) made `Hijack()` return `errHijackAlreadyWritten` whenever `Written()` was true. But `Written()` is also true when only the headers have gone out, for example after `c.Writer.WriteHeaderNow()` or `Flush()`. Some upgrade and WebSocket flows send headers and then take over the connection, and that change broke them.\n\n**The fix, in `response_writer.go`:**\n- **Nothing written yet:** hijacking is allowed, as before.\n- **Headers only:** hijacking is now allowed again.\n- **Body bytes written:** hijacking is still rejected, because taking over the connection then would corrupt the response.\n- **Error message:** changed to `\"gin: response body already written\"` to match.\n\n**Tests, in `response_writer_test.go`:** I added two cases, \"hijack after header write\" and \"hijack after flush\", and both expect success. The existing \"hijack after write should fail\" case still passes.\n\nThe changes aren't committed. Want me to commit them on a branch with the message `fix(response): refine hijack behavior for response lifecycle`?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":586,"cache_read_input_tokens":35761,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":586,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f98c069f-bc99-4437-a7c1-a14e93e10236","uuid":"8491b920-56f3-4356-b97d-2fb3197b4383","timestamp":"2026-10-18T21:28:12.398Z","request_id":"req_011CgAQrrNnpxZpFxzwroHVb"}
